{"t":1794651810,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ca082fa1-374b-4153-9516-7d1c086899fa","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-14T08:21:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 140821\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n321 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast. Mainly updated for\ncurrent conditions and a few minor tweaks in the aviation,\nmarine, and fire weather sections below. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Brisk conditions expected through this weekend with\n  scattered mountain snow showers over the Alleghenies. \n\n- 2) Dry conditions and below normal temperatures continue\n  through Tuesday with warmer temperatures mid to late next\n  week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Brisk conditions expected through this weekend \nwith scattered mountain snow showers over the Alleghenies.\n\nWith the core of an upper low set up to our northeast, flow\naloft has now turned out of the NNW. The surface low is now well\noffshore, but the pressure gradient between this and an\napproaching surface high form the west will keep winds elevated\nthroughout the day today and Sunday. Scattered snow showers are\nbeing observed early this morning with a few streamers moving\noff of Lake Erie and into our western mountains. Additional\nrounds of snow showers are expected at times through Sunday \nmorning. Highest chances for accumulation will likely be focused\nnear western Garrett County and surrounding areas, with up to \nan additional 0.5\" accumulation possible.\n\nAs the broad system gradually tracks offshore, clouds may be slow to \nclear during the weekend until the start of next week. In the wake \nof the system's stemming cold front, temperatures will be noticeably \ncolder over the next several days along with gusty winds that will \npersist through the weekend, creating overall brisk conditions \noutside. Daily highs through the weekend range in the 40s and 50s, \nwith cooler temperatures over the mountains, as well as strong \nnorthwesterly winds gusting up to 20-30 knots at times, potentially \nstronger at high elevations. Overnight, temperatures range in the \n20s and 30s. A Freeze Warning remains in effect for eastern \ncounties still in the growing season for tonight, with the 2026 \ngrowing season ending thereafter. Overall, expect nighttime \nfreezing to near-freezing temperatures to persist over the next\nseveral days, with winds diminishing sometime between late \nSunday and Monday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions and below normal temperatures \ncontinue through Tuesday with warmer temperatures mid to late next \nweek.\n\nLooking a quiescent weather pattern for much of the new workweek. No \nhazardous weather is expected with dry conditions and moderating \ntemperatures. The upper-level trough will exit east of the area\nMonday with a building upper-level ridge as surface high \npressure pushes east from the Ohio River Valley. 1032-1036 mb \nhigh pressure will meander west of the area Monday before \nshunting south toward west-central VA and southwest VA midweek. \nThe high will drift offshore Thursday into Friday leading to \nfurther warming into the upcoming weekend as the ridge continues\nto build.\n\nAs a result, expect winds decrease substantially from what we saw \nover the weekend as the high settles nearby. Temperatures will \nremain below normal under north to northwesterly flow through \nTuesday before winds shift to the west and south late next week. \nGiven the surface high nearby and lighter winds at the surface, \nexpect several nights of ample radiational cooling. This will \nlead to widespread lows near or below freezing outside of the \nurban centers and tidal shorelines. Since the growing season \nends November 15th, no additional frost/freeze headlines will be\nissued.\n\nHigh temperatures will push back into the mid to upper 50s Monday \n(40s mountains) with mid to upper 60s late next week. Dry conditions \nwill persist through the weekend despite a potential trough/low\npressure system developing in the vicinity of southeast U.S. \ncoast. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nWinds turn gusty again today, peaking up to 25-30 knots in the \nafternoon at KDCA & KIAD. Gusts up to 20 knots remain possible \nthrough Sunday afternoon before diminishing by Monday. Both\nafternoons will also see a prominent stratocu deck around 5kft\nyet again. \n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are expected Monday through Friday next \nweek. Winds will taper out of the north and northwest Monday into \nTuesday as high pressure builds into the region. Winds will switch \nback to the west and eventually south/southwest late week. Speeds \ngenerally less than 15 kts.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nGiven continued breezy conditions, have extended the SCA through\nall day Sunday, and have also added in the middle and upper\nPotomac for the whole timeframe as well starting mid-morning\ntoday. Expect wind gusts up to 20-30 knots to be persist during\nthis time. Additional SCAs may be necessary through Monday \nmorning depending on how slow winds are to diminish. Winds \ngenerally flow northwesterly through this period.\n\nSCAs will likely continue for portions of the waters though Monday \nwith sub-SCA level conditions returning Monday night into Tuesday as \nhigh pressure settles nearby. \n\nNo marine hazards are expected mid to late week. Winds will \nremain out of the north and northwest Monday into Tuesday before\nturning west to south late week at less than 15 kts.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nMuch colder air over the next few days in the wake of a departing\nlow pressure to our northeast. This will result in intermittent \nupslope snow showers in the Allegheny Mountains through Sunday \nmorning. Elsewhere, brisk winds will continue through Monday with \nwaves of clouds. Temperatures will remain below normal through \nTuesday while high pressure gradually builds overhead into the \nmiddle of next week.\n\nOver the next several days, breezy conditions will continue with \nafternoon RH values dipping into the 30s to low 40s in the valleys. \nWouldn't be shocked to see some areas drop lower, perhaps closer to \nthe upper 20s in the typical dry spots following frontal passages. \n\nWinds will begin to taper off Monday, but RHs will remain low, \nand potentially even be a bit drier, so would not be surprised if \nearly next week has a day that needs closer attention.\n\nNo rain is expected through at least the end of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Freeze Warning until 8 AM EST this morning for MDZ005-006-008-\n     016-505>508.\nVA...Freeze Warning until 8 AM EST this morning for VAZ055-056.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Freeze Warning until 8 AM EST this morning for ANZ530.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EST Sunday for ANZ530>534-\n     537>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 9 AM this morning to 6 PM EST Sunday \n     for ANZ535-536.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/EST/SRT\nAVIATION...CJL/EST/SRT\nMARINE...CJL/EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794651760,"days":{"2026-11-14":{"hi":52,"lo":39,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-15":{"hi":54,"lo":36,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":56,"lo":34,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":60,"lo":34,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":63,"lo":35,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-19":{"hi":66,"lo":37,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":66,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794650400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,4800,4800,4800,5000,5100,5300,5600,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,1